      </table-wrap>
      <fig>
        <caption><p>Plots of the default embedded cubature rules used
        for the triangle and rectangle. Cross markers show the
        <inline-formula><alternatives>
        <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{H}]]></tex-math>
        <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ℋ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
        nodes and circle markers the <inline-formula><alternatives>
        <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathsf{L}]]></tex-math>
        <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>𝖫</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
        shared nodes reused by <inline-formula><alternatives>
        <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{L}]]></tex-math>
        <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ℒ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
        color encodes the weight.
        <styled-content id="figU003Aembedded_cubature"></styled-content></p></caption>
        <graphic mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf" xlink:href="embedded_cubature.pdf" />
      </fig>
    </sec>
    <sec id="the-adaptive-algorithm">
      <title>The adaptive algorithm</title>
      <p>Given a function <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[f]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>f</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      and an initial domain <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\Omega]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      the adaptive algorithm constructs a sequence of nested partitions.
      It starts with <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{P}_0 = \{\Omega\}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      and iterates by <disp-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[
        \mathcal{P}_{n+1} = \left[ \mathcal{P}_n \setminus \left\{\omega^*\right\} \right] \cup \left\{\omega^*_1, \ldots, \omega^*_{2^d}\right \},
        \qquad \forall n \in \mathbb{N},
      ]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>\</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">{</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">}</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mo>∪</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">{</mml:mo><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msubsup><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:msup><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msubsup><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">}</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="2.0em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>∀</mml:mo><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:mi>ℕ</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula>
      where <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\omega^*]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      is chosen such that <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[E_{\omega^*} = \max \{E_\omega : \omega \in \mathcal{P}_n\}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msup></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>max</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#8289;</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>:</mml:mo><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      and <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\omega^*_1, \ldots, \omega^*_{2^d}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msubsup><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:msup><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msubsup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      are subdomains given by a partition of
      <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\omega^*]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>*</mml:mo></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
      In dimension <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      orthotopes are bisected along each axis and simplices by midpoint
      edge refinement
      (<xref alt="Gonçalves et al., 2006" rid="ref-SimplexSubdiv" ref-type="bibr">Gonçalves
      et al., 2006</xref>), both producing
      <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[2^d]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      subdomains.</p>
      <p>For the sequence <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[{(\mathcal{P}_n)}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:mi>ℕ</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      we define the global integral and error estimators
      <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{I}_n]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℐ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      and <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{E}_n]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℰ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      by <disp-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[
        \mathcal{I}_n = \sum_{\omega \in \mathcal{P}_n} I_\omega
        \quad \text{and} \quad
        \mathcal{E}_n = \sum_{\omega \in \mathcal{P}_n} E_\omega.
      ]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℐ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:munder><mml:mo>∑</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:munder><mml:msub><mml:mi>I</mml:mi><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mspace width="1.0em"></mml:mspace><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">and</mml:mtext><mml:mspace width="1.0em"></mml:mspace><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℰ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:munder><mml:mo>∑</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝒫</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:munder><mml:msub><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mi>.</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula>
      The process stops when <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{E}_n \leq \mathtt{atol}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℰ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>≤</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝚊</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚝</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚘</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚕</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      (absolute tolerance) or <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{E}_n \leq \mathtt{rtol}\,\lVert \mathcal{I}_n \rVert]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℰ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>≤</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝚛</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚝</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚘</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚕</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mspace width="0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">‖</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℐ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">‖</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      (relative tolerance), or <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[n = \mathtt{maxsubdiv}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝚖</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚊</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚡</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚜</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚞</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚋</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚍</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚒</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚟</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      (maximum number of subdivisions), and returns
      <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[(\mathcal{I}_n, \mathcal{E}_n)]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℐ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℰ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
      <fig>
        <caption><p>One level of isotropic subdivision for a triangle
        and tetrahedron split by joining edge midpoints, and a rectangle
        and cuboid bisected along each axis.
        <styled-content id="figU003Asubdivision"></styled-content></p></caption>
        <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="subdivision.png" />
      </fig>
    </sec>
  </sec>
  <sec id="implementation">
    <title>Implementation</title>
    <p>The only domain structs are <monospace>Simplex{D,T,N}</monospace>
    (with <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[N = D+1]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    vertices) and <monospace>Orthotope{D,T}</monospace> (low and high
    corners); <monospace>Segment</monospace>,
    <monospace>Triangle</monospace>, <monospace>Tetrahedron</monospace>,
    <monospace>Rectangle</monospace>, and <monospace>Cuboid</monospace>
    are type aliases, so dimension-specific defaults are selected by
    ordinary dispatch rather than run-time branching. Vertices and
    cubature nodes are <monospace>SVector</monospace>s, so the dimension
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[D]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>D</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    is part of the type and the inner evaluation loop is stack-allocated
    and specialized per <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[(D, T)]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>T</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
    Default rules are built by <monospace>@generated</monospace>
    functions, so the <monospace>EmbeddedCubature</monospace> for a
    given element type is constructed once at compile time. An
    <monospace>EmbeddedCubature</monospace> stores the shared nodes and
    separate high- and low-order weight vectors, the first
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathsf{L}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>𝖫</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    nodes being those of the low-order rule. Rules are tabulated on the
    reference domain and mapped by
    <monospace>map_from_reference</monospace>, which returns
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[(\phi, \lvert\det \operatorname{J}_\phi\rvert)]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>ϕ</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:mi>det</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#8289;</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>J</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#8289;</mml:mo><mml:mi>ϕ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">|</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    with constant Jacobian.</p>
    <p>The adaptive set lives in a max binary heap from
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/JuliaCollections/DataStructures.jl">DataStructures.jl</ext-link>,
    ordered by local error, and <monospace>integrate</monospace>
    maintains <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[(\mathcal{I}_n, \mathcal{E}_n)]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℐ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ℰ</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    incrementally. When computing many integrals of the same type, the
    heap can be pre-allocated and passed via the
    <monospace>buffer</monospace> keyword so that, provided the
    integrand does not allocate, <monospace>integrate</monospace>
    performs no allocations; see the documentation for a zero-allocation
    benchmark. Defaults are <monospace>atol = 0</monospace>,
    <monospace>rtol = sqrt(eps(T))</monospace> for element type
    <monospace>T</monospace>, and <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathtt{maxsubdiv} = 2^{13+D}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝚖</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚊</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚡</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚜</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚞</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚋</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚍</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚒</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚟</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mn>13</mml:mn><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mi>D</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
    if <monospace>maxsubdiv</monospace> is reached, a warning is issued
    and the current estimate is returned. The same code path runs with
    <monospace>Float64</monospace>, <monospace>BigFloat</monospace>, and
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/JuliaPhysics/Unitful.jl"><monospace>Unitful.jl</monospace></ext-link>
    quantities. Extensibility is provided through the
    <monospace>rule</monospace> and <monospace>norm</monospace> keywords
    on <monospace>integrate</monospace>, which let users plug in their
    own cubature and error norm.</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="extended-precision">
    <title>Extended precision</title>
    <p>As noted above, <monospace>integrate</monospace> supports
    arbitrary precision. Only the rules from Grundmann &amp; Möller
    (<xref alt="1978" rid="ref-GrundmannMoeller1978" ref-type="bibr">1978</xref>)
    and Genz &amp; Malik
    (<xref alt="1980" rid="ref-GenzMalik1980" ref-type="bibr">1980</xref>)
    are formula-based and already arbitrary-precision; the others are
    tabulated at quadruple precision and therefore incompatible with it.
    <monospace>HAdaptiveIntegration</monospace> addresses this with the
    optional <monospace>IncreasePrecisionExt</monospace> extension,
    which refines a tabulated rule’s nodes and weights by a
    least-squares Newton iteration on the polynomial-exactness (moment)
    conditions for both <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{H}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ℋ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    and <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{L}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ℒ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
    with Jacobians from <monospace>ForwardDiff.jl</monospace>
    (<xref alt="Revels et al., 2016" rid="ref-ForwardDiff2016" ref-type="bibr">Revels
    et al., 2016</xref>), following
    (<xref alt="Xiao &amp; Gimbutas, 2010, sec. 2.3" rid="ref-XiaoGimbutas2010" ref-type="bibr">Xiao
    &amp; Gimbutas, 2010, sec. 2.3</xref>). The full construction,
    including the definition of the refinement operator and its stopping
    criteria, is given in the documentation.</p>
    <p><bold>Remark.</bold> The monomial basis is convenient but poorly
    conditioned, so achieving precision <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\varepsilon]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ε</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    requires <monospace>BigFloat</monospace> arithmetic at a higher
    internal precision <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\eta < \varepsilon]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>η</mml:mi><mml:mo>&lt;</mml:mo><mml:mi>ε</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
    an <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{L}^2]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">L</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>-orthogonal
    basis would improve conditioning.</p>
    <p><bold>Remark.</bold> Symmetry is not enforced in the Newton
    iteration; a rule refined from precision
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\varepsilon]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ε</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    to <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\eta < \varepsilon]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>η</mml:mi><mml:mo>&lt;</mml:mo><mml:mi>ε</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    therefore retains its symmetry only up to
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\varepsilon]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ε</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>The repository includes API documentation, examples (buffer
  pre-allocation, <monospace>callback</monospace> mechanism, custom
  cubature, arbitrary-precision workflows), an automated test suite, and
  continuous integration covering tests, documentation, and linting.
  <monospace>HAdaptiveIntegration</monospace> has been featured in the
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://discourse.julialang.org/t/this-month-in-julia-world-2026-02/136110">Julia
  world newsletter</ext-link>, interfaced via
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/SciML/Integrals.jl">Integrals.jl</ext-link>
  (SciML ecosystem), used as a backend in Anderson et al.
  (<xref alt="2024" rid="ref-Inti" ref-type="bibr">2024</xref>), and
  adopted as a dependency of
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/JuliaGeometry/Meshes.jl/releases/tag/v0.57.0"><monospace>Meshes.jl</monospace></ext-link>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="example-gallery">
  <title>Example gallery</title>
  <p>We showcase the package on integrands with localized features
  constructed from the mollified integrand <disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[
    \rho_\delta(\psi(\boldsymbol{x})) = \frac{1}{\delta^c} \rho \left(\frac{\psi(\boldsymbol{x})}{\delta}\right),
    \qquad \rho(r) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-r^2/2},
  ]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>ρ</mml:mi><mml:mi>δ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mfrac><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:msup><mml:mi>δ</mml:mi><mml:mi>c</mml:mi></mml:msup></mml:mfrac><mml:mi>ρ</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mi>δ</mml:mi></mml:mfrac><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="2.0em"></mml:mspace><mml:mi>ρ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mfrac><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:msqrt><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>π</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msqrt></mml:mfrac><mml:msup><mml:mi>e</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo>,</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula>
  where <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\psi]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is a level-set function vanishing on <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Gamma = \psi^{-1}(0)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[c]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>c</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the codimension of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Gamma]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  so that the total mass stays <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{O}(1)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝒪</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\delta \to 0]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>δ</mml:mi><mml:mo>→</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\rho_\delta \circ \psi]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>ρ</mml:mi><mml:mi>δ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>∘</mml:mo><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  varies rapidly near <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Gamma]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  We consider three canonical geometries:</p>
  <list list-type="bullet">
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Point:</bold> <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\psi(\boldsymbol{x}) = \lVert\boldsymbol{x} - \boldsymbol{z}_0\rVert_2]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">‖</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝐳</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">‖</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      codimension <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[c = d]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      (point refinement);</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Hypersphere:</bold> <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\psi(\boldsymbol{x}) = \lVert\boldsymbol{x} - \boldsymbol{z}_1\rVert_2^2 - r^2]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">‖</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝐳</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">‖</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      codimension <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[c = 1]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      (curve/surface refinement);</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Hyperplane:</bold> <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\psi(\boldsymbol{x}) = x_1 - a]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ψ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐱</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mi>a</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
      codimension <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[c = 1]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      (axis-aligned hyperplane refinement).</p>
    </list-item>
  </list>
  <p>We set <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\delta = 0.05]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>δ</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.05</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\boldsymbol{z}_0 = \tfrac{1}{\pi}(1, \ldots, 1)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝐳</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mstyle displaystyle="false"><mml:mfrac><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mi>π</mml:mi></mml:mfrac></mml:mstyle><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\boldsymbol{z}_1 = (1, 0, \ldots, 0)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>𝐳</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r = 2/3]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[a = 1/\pi]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>a</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mi>π</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
  the axis-aligned hyperplane benchmarks isotropic versus
  estimator-driven subdivision. Convergence plots sweep
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathtt{rtol} = 10^{-i}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝚛</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚝</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚘</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚕</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  (<inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[i = 1, \ldots, 10]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>10</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
  up to <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[8]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mn>8</mml:mn></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  in 3D), recording the total number of evaluations
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  the returned error estimate, and the actual error against a reference
  solution computed at <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathtt{rtol} = 10^{-12}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝚛</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚝</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚘</mml:mi><mml:mi>𝚕</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>12</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
  <p>When one evaluation of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[f]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>f</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  dominates the per-node overhead of the algorithm (a few clock cycles),
  the wall-clock time is roughly <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  times the cost of a single evaluation, so
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is a hardware-independent proxy for runtime.</p>
  <sec id="simplices">
    <title>Simplices</title>
    <p><xref alt="[fig:cvg_simplex]" rid="figU003Acvg_simplex">[fig:cvg_simplex]</xref>
    shows convergence on the unit triangle
    (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[d=2]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
    Radon-Laurie rule
    (<xref alt="Laurie, 1982" rid="ref-Laurie1982" ref-type="bibr">Laurie,
    1982</xref>)) and tetrahedron (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[d=3]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
    Grundmann-Möller rule
    (<xref alt="Grundmann &amp; Möller, 1978" rid="ref-GrundmannMoeller1978" ref-type="bibr">Grundmann
    &amp; Möller, 1978</xref>), available in arbitrary dimensions). Two
    observations hold across all features and both geometries: the
    estimated error reliably tracks the actual error, confirming a sound
    a posteriori indicator; and once the feature is resolved, errors
    follow <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathcal{O}(N^{-(k+1)/d})]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>𝒪</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    with <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[k = k_h]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mi>h</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    or <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[k_\ell]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mi>ℓ</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    and the exponent implied by <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[N \propto h^{-d}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mo>∝</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>h</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
    <graphic mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf" xlink:href="cvg_triangle.pdf" />
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Convergence of the actual and estimated errors for the
      point, hypersphere, and hyperplane features on the unit triangle
      (top) and unit tetrahedron (bottom) versus the number of
      evaluations (<inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>).
      Insets show the sub-domains and the integrand as max-intensity
      projections onto the coordinate planes.
      <styled-content id="figU003Acvg_simplex"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf" xlink:href="cvg_tetrahedron.pdf" />
    </fig>
  </sec>
  <sec id="orthotopes-and-comparison-with-hcubature.jl">
    <title>Orthotopes and comparison with
    <monospace>HCubature.jl</monospace></title>
    <p><xref alt="[fig:cvg_orthotope]" rid="figU003Acvg_orthotope">[fig:cvg_orthotope]</xref>
    compares <monospace>HAdaptiveIntegration</monospace> against
    <monospace>HCubature.jl</monospace> on the unit square and cube. In
    2D, both use the Genz-Malik rule
    (<xref alt="Genz &amp; Malik, 1980" rid="ref-GenzMalik1980" ref-type="bibr">Genz
    &amp; Malik, 1980</xref>), isolating the subdivision strategy; in
    3D, the cubature rules also differ (Berntsen-Espelid
    (<xref alt="Berntsen &amp; Espelid, 1988" rid="ref-BerntsenEspelid1988" ref-type="bibr">Berntsen
    &amp; Espelid, 1988</xref>) for
    <monospace>HAdaptiveIntegration</monospace>). For the point and
    hypersphere features the solvers are comparable in both dimensions.
    For the hyperplane, <monospace>HCubature.jl</monospace> has a clear
    advantage because its estimator refines exclusively along
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[x_1]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    rather than bisecting uniformly in all
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    directions, and this gap grows with <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
    motivating anisotropic splitting as future work.</p>
    <graphic mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf" xlink:href="cvg_rectangle.pdf" />
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Convergence of the actual and estimated errors for the
      point, hypersphere, and hyperplane features on the unit square
      (top) and the unit cube (bottom), comparing
      <monospace>HAdaptiveIntegration</monospace> (HAI) with
      <monospace>HCubature.jl</monospace>. Insets show the sub-domains
      and the integrand as max-intensity projections onto the coordinate
      planes.
      <styled-content id="figU003Acvg_orthotope"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf" xlink:href="cvg_cuboid.pdf" />
    </fig>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgments">
  <title>Acknowledgments</title>
  <p>We thank the reviewers and editors for their time and comments,
  which helped improve both the package and this paper.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>Generative AI was used to help develop the code and to draft and
  edit parts of this manuscript. All text produced with AI assistance
  was reviewed, revised, and verified by the authors before inclusion in
  the code or paper.</p>
</sec>
</body>
